{
  "id": "COMPOUND:3689 - PROTEIN:730",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a mechanistic link between 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline and the Anti-Muellerian hormone type-2 receptor, mediated through the enzyme K-252a. This relationship is supported by multiple independent pathways, all of which converge on K-252a as a central mediator. The drug targets three distinct proteins—Phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta, Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase [isomerizing], and HSVEC—each of which is targeted by K-252a and ultimately interacts with the Anti-Muellerian hormone type-2 receptor. The consistent scores across these paths indicate a robust and reliable mechanism, with K-252a acting as a bridge between the drug and the receptor. The presence of multiple targets and their shared interaction with K-252a supports a unified pathway, suggesting that the drug may exert its effects through a common biochemical mechanism involving K-252a. The evidence is consistent and does not require additional layers of complexity, reinforcing the hypothesis that the drug's interaction with the receptor is mediated through this enzyme.",
